### Phase 9: BUG — personfradrag counted twice on a mid-period raise
- `SalaryEstimateService._combine_estimates` (apps/payroll/services.py) sums the *tax breakdown* field by field, including `monthly_deduction`. So when one payroll period holds two term sets (a mid-period raise, or a mid-period start/end), the monthly personfradrag is subtracted once per term set → tax too low → **net pay overstated**.
- Only bites when two term sets share one period. A single-term-set period returns `ests[0]` untouched and is correct.
- Affects everything built on it: the dashboard (`_compute_pay`, which for the *hourly* path also estimates earned and planned separately — same double-count), the workplace page, the payslip editor, and analytics' `_compose_row`.
- The **earned/planned** half of this is already fixed in analytics (v0.23.0): a period is estimated once and the bands allocated from that total, so only the multi-term-set case is left there. The dashboard still splits earned/planned into two estimates.
- Right fix: combine the *bases* (taxable gross, employee pension, ATP) and run `TaxCalculationService.calculate` **once** on the combined basis, instead of summing per-term-set tax breakdowns. Gross is already correct — it is linear — so only tax/net move.
- Deliberately left out of the v0.23.0 analytics rework: fixing it changes numbers on three other pages, so it wants its own commit + its own regression tests.



## Platform creation

- Make the basis of this app into a base platform such that other Bit-apps can be made with a similar visual identity and user experience and the same deep set of features and integrations.
- Save it to D:\Github\BitBase\
- The idea is that any platform upgrade made in the individual apps would also be made as a pull request to the base repository, so that all apps may benefit from them. 
- Make sure to note this down in readme and in CLAUDE.md so that Claude automatically will think about if what we are doing now could be considered a platform upgrade, and if I would like them to draft up a pull request and send it off to the base platform repo.


